<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  WiX Burn bootstrapper wrapping smtprelayd.wxs's plain per-machine MSI.

  Why this exists: msiexec.exe's own "MSI_LUA" compatibility shim forces
  CLIENTUILEVEL=None for a UAC-split-token Administrator running a
  maintenance operation (uninstall/repair) on an already admin-assigned
  per-machine product, decided client-side, before the MSI is even opened,
  so nothing in smtprelayd.wxs itself can affect it (see PROGRESS.md,
  2026-08-21, twenty-ninth session). This bundle's own manifest requests
  elevation once, up front; by the time it reaches the chained MsiPackage
  below, the calling process is already fully elevated, so msiexec never
  needs to invoke that shim, and the plain MSI's own UI (PurgeDataDlg,
  ExitDialog) renders normally, including through Apps & Features, since
  Windows registers *this* bundle's own uninstall command there once it is
  what gets installed, not the raw MSI directly.

  Built with WiX Toolset v3 (candle.exe -ext WixBalExtension, then light.exe
  -ext WixBalExtension), the same runner-preinstalled toolset smtprelayd.wxs
  already uses. No signing step exists in this project, so the usual
  insignia.exe/burn.exe detach-reattach is not needed here; that is only
  required to Authenticode-sign the Burn engine stub separately from the
  outer bundle exe.

  DisplayInternalUI="yes" on the chained MsiPackage was first thought to let
  the wrapped MSI show its own authored UI (PurgeDataDlg, ExitDialog)
  directly through the bundle. Wrong, confirmed from a real uninstall's
  verbose log: Burn only ever grants a chained MsiPackage Basic UI
  (CLIENTUILEVEL=3), never Full, no matter what DisplayInternalUI is set to;
  Basic suppresses every package-authored dialog, custom or stock, so
  neither PurgeDataDlg nor ExitDialog ever rendered through the bundle,
  either way. Left "yes" regardless: it still lets the MSI's own ActionText
  feed Burn's progress page instead of a blank "Processing..." label.

  The purge-data question now lives in the bundle's own UI instead, via
  smtprelayd-bundle-theme.xml's CLEANDATA checkbox on the Modify page (the
  page WixStdBA shows for Apps & Features' Uninstall, since the bundle is
  already installed; see that file's own header comment for how the
  checkbox-to-Bundle-variable binding works. CLEANDATA is still forwarded
  into the wrapped MSI's property table via <MsiProperty> below exactly as
  before, so both paths keep working:
    interactive: the Modify page's checkbox sets the Bundle variable, which
      MsiProperty then forwards into the MSI's own CLEANDATA property
    scripted: smtprelayd-<version>-amd64-setup.exe /uninstall /quiet
      CLEANDATA=1 still works; the Modify page (and its checkbox) is never
      visited for an explicit /uninstall, so this remains the only way to
      purge data from a non-interactive uninstall, unchanged from before
      this bundle existed.

  Variables supplied at candle.exe time (-d<Name>=<Value>):
    Version    Bundle version, same numeric value smtprelayd.wxs uses
    MsiPath    path to the already-built smtprelayd-<version>-amd64.msi
    ThemeFile  path to smtprelayd-bundle-theme.xml, passed explicitly
               rather than as a bare relative filename, since ThemeFile's
               own path resolution relative to the invocation's working
               directory is not something to leave implicit
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/wix/2006/wi"
     xmlns:bal="http://schemas.microsoft.com/wix/BalExtension">

  <Bundle Name="smtprelayd"
          Version="$(var.Version)"
          Manufacturer="Tokajer"
          UpgradeCode="2ab0d134-9ff4-49c4-bf86-1c63c7f27540">

    <BootstrapperApplicationRef Id="WixStandardBootstrapperApplication.HyperlinkLicense">
      <bal:WixStandardBootstrapperApplication
        LicenseUrl="https://github.com/tokajer/smtprelayd/blob/main/LICENSE"
        ThemeFile="$(var.ThemeFile)"
        SuppressOptionsUI="yes" />
    </BootstrapperApplicationRef>

    <!-- Type="numeric": WixStdBA's checkbox-to-variable binding reads and
         writes this via BalGetNumericVariable/SetVariableNumeric, not a
         string comparison. Overridable so an operator can still pass
         CLEANDATA=1 on the bundle's own command line for a scripted purge. -->
    <Variable Name="CLEANDATA" Type="numeric" Value="0" bal:Overridable="yes" />

    <Chain>
      <MsiPackage SourceFile="$(var.MsiPath)" DisplayInternalUI="yes" Vital="yes">
        <MsiProperty Name="CLEANDATA" Value="[CLEANDATA]" />
      </MsiPackage>
    </Chain>

  </Bundle>
</Wix>
